solution_str: To find the inverse of \(\mathbf{A}^3\), we need to use the property of matrix inverses that \((\mathbf{A}^3)^{-1} = (\mathbf{A}^{-1})^3\).

Given \(\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 8 \\ -2 & -5 \end{pmatrix}\), we need to compute \((\mathbf{A}^{-1})^3\).

To find \(\mathbf{B}^2\) where \(\mathbf{B} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 8 \\ -2 & -5 \end{pmatrix}\), we calculate \(\mathbf{B} \cdot \mathbf{B}\):

\[
\mathbf{B}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 8 \\ -2 & -5 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 8 \\ -2 & -5 \end{pmatrix}
\]

We will perform the matrix multiplication step-by-step:

\[
\mathbf{B}^2 = \begin{pmatrix}
(3 \cdot 3 + 8 \cdot -2) & (3 \cdot 8 + 8 \cdot -5) \\
(-2 \cdot 3 + -5 \cdot -2) & (-2 \cdot 8 + -5 \cdot -5)
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Calculate each element:

\[
\mathbf{B}^2 = \begin{pmatrix}
(3 \cdot 3 + 8 \cdot -2) & (3 \cdot 8 + 8 \cdot -5) \\
(-2 \cdot 3 + -5 \cdot -2) & (-2 \cdot 8 + -5 \cdot -5)
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
(9 - 16) & (24 - 40) \\
(-6 + 10) & (-16 + 25)
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
-7 & -16 \\
4 & 9
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Next, we need to find \(\mathbf{B}^3\), which is \(\mathbf{B}^2 \cdot \mathbf{B}\):

\[
\mathbf{B}^3 = \begin{pmatrix} -7 & -16 \\ 4 & 9 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 8 \\ -2 & -5 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Calculate each element:

\[
\mathbf{B}^3 = \begin{pmatrix}
(-7 \cdot 3 + -16 \cdot -2) & (-7 \cdot 8 + -16 \cdot -5) \\
(4 \cdot 3 + 9 \cdot -2) & (4 \cdot 8 + 9 \cdot -5)
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Calculate each expression:

\[
\mathbf{B}^3 = \begin{pmatrix}
(-21 + 32) & (-56 + 80) \\
(12 - 18) & (32 - 45)
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
11 & 24 \\
-6 & -13
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Thus, the inverse of \(\mathbf{A}^3\) is:

\[
(\mathbf{A}^3)^{-1} = \mathbf{B}^3 = \begin{pmatrix} 11 & 24 \\ -6 & -13 \end{pmatrix}
\]

So, the final answer is:

\[
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 11 & 24 \\ -6 & -13 \end{pmatrix}}
\], ground_truth: \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 0 \\ -66 & -137 \end{pmatrix}
