The area of the semi-circle with diameter \( AB \) is \( \frac{1}{2} \pi a^2 \). Similarly, the areas of the semi-circles with diameters \( AC \) and \( CB \) are \( \frac{1}{2} \pi b^2 \) and \( \frac{1}{2} \pi c^2 \) respectively. The total shaded area is the area of the semi-circle with diameter \( AB \) minus the areas of the semi-circles with diameters \( AC \) and \( CB \):

\[
\text{Shaded Area} = \frac{1}{2} \pi a^2 - \left( \frac{1}{2} \pi b^2 + \frac{1}{2} \pi c^2 \right) = \frac{1}{2} \pi (a^2 - b^2 - c^2) = \frac{1}{2} \pi (a^2 - b^2 - (a+b)^2) = \frac{1}{2} \pi (a^2 - b^2 - a^2 - 2ab - b^2) = \frac{1}{2} \pi (-2b^2 - 2ab) = -\pi (b^2 + ab).
\]

Since \( c = a + b \), we have \( c^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab \). The area of the circular sector with radius \( CD \), which is the height from \( C \) perpendicular to \( AB \), is \( \pi (CD)^2 \). Since \( CD = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \), the area of the circle with radius \( CD \) is \( \pi (a^2 + b^2) \).

The ratio of the shaded area to the area of the circle with radius \( CD \) is:

\[
