Given a right triangle ABC with <A = 90°, the bisectors of angles B and C meet at I, and IH is perpendicular to BC at H. We are given HB = 5 cm and HC = 8 cm. We need to find the area of triangle ABC.

Since I is the incenter of the triangle, the incenter is equidistant from all sides. The point H is the foot of the perpendicular from I to BC, so IH is the inradius (r) of the triangle. Given HB = 5 cm and HC = 8 cm, we can find BC as BC = HB + HC = 5 + 8 = 13 cm.

The incenter I is located at a distance r from each side. Therefore, AB + AC - BC = 2r. This is derived from the formula for the inradius of a right triangle, which is \( r = \frac{AB + AC - BC}{2} \).

We also know that the area of the triangle can be expressed as \( \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times AB \times AC \) and also as \( \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times BC \times r \). We can use these relationships to find the area.

