This leads us to the condition that the rectangle can be dissected into two similar but incongruent polygons if and only if $k \neq 1$. If $k=1$, the rectangle becomes a square, and any dissection would result in congruent polygons, as any similar dissection would maintain the same side lengths.

Therefore, the values of $k$ for which it is possible to dissect a $1 \times k$ rectangle into two similar but incongruent polygons are all $k > 0$ except $k=1$.
