Let's start with \( n = 1 \):
- \( 2 \cdot 1 + 1 = 3 \) (3 is a prime number, so \( n = 1 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 2 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 2 + 1 = 5 \) (5 is a prime number, so \( n = 2 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 3 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 3 + 1 = 7 \) (7 is a prime number, so \( n = 3 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 4 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 4 + 1 = 9 \) (9 is composite)
  - \( 3 \cdot 4 + 1 = 13 \) (13 is a prime number, so \( n = 4 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 5 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 5 + 1 = 11 \) (11 is a prime number, so \( n = 5 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 6 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 6 + 1 = 13 \) (13 is a prime number, so \( n = 6 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 7 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 7 + 1 = 15 \) (15 is composite)
  - \( 3 \cdot 7 + 1 = 22 \) (22 is composite)
  - \( 4 \cdot 7 + 1 = 29 \) (29 is a prime number, so \( n = 7 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 8 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 8 + 1 = 17 \) (17 is a prime number, so \( n = 8 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 9 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 9 + 1 = 19 \) (19 is a prime number, so \( n = 9 \) is not lucky)
- Next, \( n = 10 \):
  - \( 2 \cdot 10 + 1 = 21 \) (21 is composite)
  - \( 3 \cdot 10 + 1 = 31 \) (31 is a prime number, so \( n = 10 \) is not lucky)
