1. The discriminant \(\Delta = b^2 - 4c > 0\).
2. The roots are positive, which implies both \(b > 0\), \(c > 0\), and \(b^2 > 4c\).

For the equation to not have two distinct positive real roots, at least one of these conditions must not be satisfied. We will count the number of valid \((b, c)\) pairs and then calculate the probability.

First, let's calculate the total number of possible \((b, c)\) pairs. Since \(b\) and \(c\) can each take any integer value from \(-5\) to \(5\) (inclusive), there are \(11 \times 11 = 121\) possible pairs.

Next, we will count the number of pairs \((b, c)\) for which the quadratic equation \(x^2 + bx + c = 0\) has two distinct positive roots. We will use the conditions \(b^2 > 4c\), \(b > 0\), and \(c > 0\).
