First, we need to evaluate $\cos \alpha = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$. We know that the cosine of 30 degrees is $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$, so $\alpha = 30^\circ$.

When a regular hexagon is rotated by 30 degrees about one of its vertices, the overlap region will be a kite-shaped area formed by two equilateral triangles. Each side of the hexagon is 1, so the kite will consist of two 30-60-90 triangles.

In a 30-60-90 triangle, the sides are in the ratio $1 : \sqrt{3} : 2$. The side opposite the 30 degree angle is half the hypotenuse, so for a hexagon of side length 1, the altitude (which is also the height of the 30-60-90 triangle) is $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.

The area of one of these 30-60-90 triangles is:
\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times 1 \times \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}. \]

Since the kite is made up of two such triangles, the area of the kite (shaded region) is:
\[ 2 \times \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}. \]
</think>

