Suppose we have a right triangle with \(a = 10\) (hypotenuse), \(\gamma = 30^\circ\) (angle), and \(c\) (opposite the angle \(\gamma\)). The length of the leg \(c\) is given by \(c = a \cdot \sin \gamma = 10 \cdot \sin 30^\circ = 10 \cdot 0.5 = 5\). Using the incorrect formula \(c = \frac{a}{\sin \gamma}\) would give \(c = \frac{10}{\sin 30^\circ} = \frac{10}{0.5} = 20\), which is clearly not the length of the leg \(c\).

