\[
\begin{array}{cccc}
& a & b & c \\
& d & e & f \\
+ & g & h & i \\
\hline
1 & 6 & 6 & 5 \\
\end{array}
\]

This means that:

\[
1665 = (100a + 10b + c) + (100d + 10e + f) + (100g + 10h + i)
\]

First, let's consider the units digit. The units digit of the sum 1665 is 5, so:

\[
c + f + i \equiv 5 \pmod{10}
\]

Next, let's consider the tens digit. The tens digit of the sum is 6, so the carry from the units column plus the tens digits must add up to either 6 or 16 (since we might have a carry from the units column). Let's denote the carry from the units column by \(x\), so:

\[
(c + f + i + x) \mod 10 = 6
\]

This means:

\[
c + f + i + x = 6 \text{ or } 16
\]

We know \(c + f + i \equiv 5 \pmod{10}\), so:

\[
