solution_str: To determine the total height of the stack of washers, we need to find the number of washers in the stack and the thickness of each washer.

First, identify the number of washers in the stack. The outside diameter of the top washer is 10 cm and it decreases by 0.5 cm for each subsequent washer until the outside diameter is 2 cm. We can set up the following equation to find the number of washers \( n \):

\[ 10 - 0.5(n-1) = 2 \]

Simplify and solve for \( n \):

\[ 10 - 0.5n + 0.5 = 2 \]
\[ 10.5 - 0.5n = 2 \]
\[ 10.5 - 2 = 0.5n \]
\[ 8.5 = 0.5n \]
\[ n = \frac{8.5}{0.5} \]
\[ n = 17 \]

So, there are 17 washers in the stack. Now, we need to find the thickness of each washer. The thickness of the top washer is 1 cm and it decreases by 0.1 cm for each subsequent washer until the thickness is 0 cm (since the smallest washer is 2 cm in diameter and the washers are just touching, the thickness of the smallest washer is 0). However, the thickness of the layer of metal (the washer) is the average thickness of the washer, which is half the height of the washer.

The thickness of the top washer is 1 cm, the second washer is 0.9 cm, the third is 0.8 cm, and so on. The thickness of the \( k \)-th washer is \( 1 - 0.1(k-1) \). The total height of the stack is the sum of the thicknesses of all the washers:

\[ \text{Total height} = \sum_{k=1}^{17} \left(1 - 0.1(k-1)\right) \]
\[ \text{Total height} = \sum_{k=1}^{17} \left(1 - 0.1k + 0.1\right) \]
\[ \text{Total height} = \sum_{k=1}^{17} \left(1.1 - 0.1k\right) \]
\[ \text{Total height} = \sum_{k=1}^{17} 1.1 - \sum_{k=1}^{17} 0.1k \]
\[ \text{Total height} = 1.1 \times 17 - 0.1 \sum_{k=1}^{17} k \]
\[ \text{Total height} = 18.7 - 0.1 \left(\frac{17 \times 18}{2}\right) \]
\[ \text{Total height} = 18.7 - 0.1 \times 153 \]
\[ \text{Total height} = 18.7 - 15.3 \]
\[ \text{Total height} = 3.4 \]

Therefore, the total height of the stack from the bottom of the bottom washer to the top of the top washer is \(\boxed{3.4}\) cm., ground_truth: 11.9
