The volume of water in the first cylinder is:
\[V_1 = \pi (2^2)(8) = 32\pi \text{ cm}^3\]

We need to find the depth of the water in the second cylinder when all of this water is poured into it. The volume of water remains the same, so \(32\pi \text{ cm}^3\) of water will fill the second cylinder up to a certain depth. Let's call this depth \(h_2\). The volume of water in the second cylinder after pouring will be:
\[V_2 = \pi (4^2) h_2 = 16\pi h_2\]

Since the volume of water is the same, we can set \(V_1 = V_2\):
\[32\pi = 16\pi h_2\]
