     (5, 1, 4, 3, 2, 0) \rightarrow (1, 5, 4, 3, 2, 0)
     \]
     Here, we can't get to \((1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 0)\) because we need to move \(2, 3, 4\) before \(5\).

From the above analysis, it seems that for \( n \geq 2 \), we can't get to \((1, 2, 3, \ldots, n, 0)\) from \((1, n, n-1, \ldots, 3, 2, 0)\). The only case that works is \( n = 1 \).

### Conclusion
The only \( n \) for which we can obtain \((1, 2, \ldots, n, 0)\) from \((1, n, n-1, \ldots, 3, 2, 0)\) is \( n = 1 \).

