We will test each of the given values of the right side of the equation \(4n + 9\):

1. For \(4n + 9 = -1\):
\[4n = -1 - 9 \Rightarrow 4n = -10 \Rightarrow n = -\frac{10}{4} = -\frac{5}{2}\]
This is not an integer.

2. For \(4n + 9 = 1\):
\[4n = 1 - 9 \Rightarrow 4n = -8 \Rightarrow n = -\frac{8}{4} = -2\]
This is an integer.

3. For \(4n + 9 = -3\):
\[4n = -3 - 9 \Rightarrow 4n = -12 \Rightarrow n = -\frac{12}{4} = -3\]
This is an integer.

4. For \(4n + 9 = 3\):
\[4n = 3 - 9 \Rightarrow 4n = -6 \Rightarrow n = -\frac{6}{4} = -\frac{3}{2}\]
This is not an integer.

From the above calculations, the only integer solutions for \(n\) are \(n = -2\) and \(n = -3\).

Therefore, the values of \(n\) that satisfy the condition are \(-2\) and \(-3\). There are exactly 2 values.
