Firstly, for every positive integer \( n \) and odd positive integer \( a < 2^n \), there exists an odd positive integer \( b < 2^n \) such that \( f\left( \frac{a}{2^n} \right) = \frac{b}{2^n} \). This implies that \( f \) maps dyadic rationals (numbers of the form \( \frac{a}{2^n} \)) to dyadic rationals, and more specifically, it maps odd dyadic rationals to odd dyadic rationals.

Since \( f \) is differentiable with a continuous derivative, and given the condition above, we can use the Mean Value Theorem on small intervals. Specifically, if we consider the interval \([1/2 - \epsilon, 1/2 + \epsilon]\), for any \(\epsilon > 0\), there exist points \( x_1, x_2 \) in \([1/2 - \epsilon, 1/2 + \epsilon]\) such that \( x_1 \) and \( x_2 \) are odd dyadic rationals and \( x_2 - x_1 \) is very small (i.e., \( x_1 \) and \( x_2 \) are close to \(1/2\)).

By the Mean Value Theorem, for \( x_1 \neq x_2 \) in the interval, there exists a point \( c \in (x_1, x_2) \) such that:
\[ f'(c) = \frac{f(x_2) - f(x_1)}{x_2 - x_1} \]

