A landmark point \( p_l \) exists if \( 2 \le l \le n-1 \) and \((p_{l-1} - p_l)(p_{l+1} - p_l) > 0\). This means that \( p_{l-1} \) and \( p_l \) must either (1) both be greater than \( p_l \) or (2) both be less than \( p_l \).

For a permutation of \( \{1, 2, \ldots, n\} \) to have exactly one landmark point, the permutation must be either:
1. Increasing up to the landmark point and then decreasing.
2. Decreasing up to the landmark point and then increasing.

For a given \( n \), if we let the landmark point be at \( p_l \), the sequence before \( p_l \) must be either strictly increasing or strictly decreasing, and similarly, the sequence after \( p_l \) must be the opposite (strictly decreasing or strictly increasing).

For each position \( l \) (where \( 2 \le l \le n-1 \)), there are exactly 2 possible permutations (one for each of the above cases) that have exactly one landmark point at \( p_l \). So, there are \( (n-2) \times 2 = 2(n-2) \) such permutations for each \( l \).

Therefore, \( L(n) = 2(n-2) \).

