The second derivative of \( g(x) \) is:
\[ g''(x) = -3e^{-x}(2x^2 - 2x - 2) = -3e^{-x} \cdot 2(x^2 - x - 1). \]

Evaluating at \( x = \sqrt{2} \):
\[ g''(\sqrt{2}) = -3e^{-\sqrt{2}} \cdot 2((\sqrt{2})^2 - \sqrt{2} - 1) = -3e^{-\sqrt{2}} \cdot 2(2 - \sqrt{2} - 1) = -3e^{-\sqrt{2}} \cdot 2(1 - \sqrt{2}) < 0, \]
so \( x = \sqrt{2} \) is a maximum.

Evaluating at \( x = -\sqrt{2} \):
\[ g''(-\sqrt{2}) = -3e^{\sqrt{2}} \cdot 2((- \sqrt{2})^2 + \sqrt{2} - 1) = -3e^{\sqrt{2}} \cdot 2(2 + \sqrt{2} - 1) = -3e^{\sqrt{2}} \cdot 2(1 + \sqrt{2}) > 0, \]
so \( x = -\sqrt{2} \) is a minimum.

