A tetrahedron is a polyhedron with 4 vertices. If a plane \(\alpha\) is a median plane, the distance from each vertex to the plane is equal. For a tetrahedron, the only plane that is equidistant from all four vertices is the plane that passes through the centroid of the tetrahedron and is parallel to the plane formed by the midpoints of the diagonals of the tetrahedron (a plane that bisects the tetrahedron into two equal-volume halves). This plane is unique for any given tetrahedron. Therefore, a tetrahedron has exactly one median plane.

