A tetrahedron has 4 vertices. A plane that is equidistant from all 4 vertices must be the plane that intersects the centroid of the tetrahedron and is parallel to the plane formed by the midpoints of the opposite edges. For a tetrahedron, there is exactly one such plane. This plane is the plane that bisects the tetrahedron into two equal volumes. Therefore, a tetrahedron has exactly one median plane.
\[
\text{Number of median planes of a tetrahedron} = 1
\]

### (2) Median Planes of a Parallelepiped
A parallelepiped has 8 vertices. A plane that is equidistant from all 8 vertices must be the plane that passes through the center of the parallelepiped and is parallel to the plane formed by the midpoints of the edges in the plane perpendicular to the plane of the median plane. This plane can be either the plane parallel to the face of the parallelepiped or the plane that bisects the parallelepiped into two equal volumes. In a parallelepiped, there are exactly 3 such planes:
